  "worked_solution": "We can just take the constant factor out of the integral, and then apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2}{5}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\frac{2}{5}\\int 1\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}\\int {y}^{0}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{0+1}}{0+1}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot y+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}y+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, we can see that for any constant $k,$ we have $\\int k\\,\\text{d}x=kx+C.$",
